{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789241134,"nonce":"OjPLJWeuiW6RmQxeGRgo1vIN82M=","title":"Drop-site analysis released a box a library function had stored","message":"A pointer parameter with no stated ownership is Borrowed, and the drop\npass releases a caller's fresh allocation after the call. The shared\nbuilt-in library's `zb_list_set_*` and `zb_list_insert_*` store their\nelement argument in the list, so a box created and stored through them\nwas freed by the caller and the list held a dangling pointer. Surfaced\nas a closure cell reading a garbage integer after `count = 10`.\n\nFix: the library's DSL has `owned(name, ty)` for a parameter the\nfunction keeps, which lowers to `ParamOwnership::Owned`, and the Python\nfrontend declares every heap-typed parameter of a Python function Owned\nbecause Python code may keep anything it is handed. The cost is that a\ncaller never releases a box it passes to a Python function; a collector\nor an escape analysis over the callee body would recover that.","files":null}]}